# Jarvis heavy-lane runner: drop privilege to a project's own unix user.
#
# install -m 0440 deploy/jarvis-runner.sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/jarvis-runner
#
# This is the whole of the runner's elevated authority, and it is deliberately
# one command with a fixed shape:
#
#   * the binary is named absolutely, so PATH cannot be used to substitute it;
#   * --reuid and --regid may only name jarvis-p-* users, never root and never
#     jarvis itself;
#   * --clear-groups is mandatory in the pattern, so the harness cannot inherit
#     the runner's group memberships — which are what let the RUNNER reach every
#     project directory;
#   * only the harness binary may be exec'd afterwards.
#
# sudoers patterns are fnmatch globs. A character class was tried first and
# `visudo -c` rejected it, so the targets are matched with a plain `*` — which is
# no weaker than it looks: sudo matches argv ELEMENT BY ELEMENT, so a wildcard
# inside one argument cannot introduce another argument, and `--reuid=jarvis-p-*`
# can only ever be a single argument beginning with `jarvis-p-`. The trailing `*`
# on the harness arguments is unavoidable — it takes a prompt — which is why the
# binary itself is pinned absolutely.
Defaults:jarvis !requiretty
jarvis ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/setpriv --reuid=jarvis-p-* --regid=jarvis-p-* --clear-groups -- /usr/local/bin/claude *
jarvis ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/setpriv --reuid=jarvis-p-* --regid=jarvis-p-* --clear-groups -- /usr/bin/node *
